#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198558 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b2bbcab-d62a-5075-8355-9faf937b3b64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193980} {:answer/id #uuid "978e1fe9-4852-59e9-8773-0093fead0b33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193981} {:answer/id #uuid "9b10dc32-4033-5b4f-b063-1d903f627bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193982} {:answer/id #uuid "c43a6e0c-13f2-5e06-a652-597bacfa0c1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193983} {:answer/id #uuid "3bb45f0e-f0a4-5b16-a97f-a59ee8482aa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193984}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b10dc32-4033-5b4f-b063-1d903f627bfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193982} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf08e281-72a3-5471-856b-830ab0dddba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186193979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c7922961-2d09-5acf-9285-b8bc18d81065" :question/math-academy-id "q-126828" :question/problem "Given the vector field $F(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. $∇\\cdot F=0$\n\nII. $F$ is a source-free vector field\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0$ along any piecewise-smooth curve $C$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nNote that $F(x,y)=x\\,i-y\\,j$ is a vector field with the simply connected domain $D={R}^{2},$ where $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=-y.$ Computing the partial derivatives, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, we have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=1+(-1)=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. This follows from statement I.\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $F$ is source-free, the result ${\\int }_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0$ is guaranteed for closed curves only.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198625 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "939e78ae-4e2c-5ec0-a6f9-cb84365bf9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194383} {:answer/id #uuid "ca03740f-54af-5ef3-9423-bef53dce28f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194384} {:answer/id #uuid "1a5b4489-073e-52d1-b96b-4d6a8829b185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194385} {:answer/id #uuid "c3a073ff-7b82-5cb3-a3ab-85a3bfc8115e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516525} {:answer/id #uuid "e94e1e83-b34b-5c19-b820-1bb79fe623cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "939e78ae-4e2c-5ec0-a6f9-cb84365bf9ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194383} :answer-field/id #uuid "36a3e99a-fa1b-516e-b9ce-8e1cc0722587" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194381}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e2446c5e-a91d-56d6-9926-c800e872256d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162216" :question/problem "![](images/4f/4f9cd7d9e173410de891756ee2ef260a16677629dacbfc56ab5e6e207a9e40e4.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,i+2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198368 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c2dcf4be-ea5a-5ab3-8919-e61da330fa85" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding Source-Free Vector Fields"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f038cab-9126-5da7-9160-6ef0ca5d9c7c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516171} {:answer/id #uuid "80c57ef5-3745-587d-8aa3-f8f37a48d08f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516383} {:answer/id #uuid "a789a160-6d54-5ae6-bea1-64c85a41f536" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Not enough information" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516461} {:answer/id #uuid "c7a6dd0f-1b6a-5d10-8dec-872aaf97ae8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516534} {:answer/id #uuid "e865d1e4-4903-5b92-a7e7-c03d0e7b2809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516594}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e865d1e4-4903-5b92-a7e7-c03d0e7b2809" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516594} :answer-field/id #uuid "383d60a8-2215-591e-977a-5ba213748bef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "443c8318-6bcc-5f69-a92f-89796fbae407" :question/math-academy-id "q-162553" :question/problem "![](images/0c/0c7d224dce33a51b94aa81c11ac4fae923c294e61372eb12d77e3656a0f61489.png)\n\nWhat is the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,j$ across the curve $C$ shown above?\n\n*Hint: Start by checking whether $F$ is source-free.*" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C⊂D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}⊂D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡y+\\operatorname{cos}⁡y \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $F$ is a source-free vector field. So, the flux of $F$ across any closed curve is zero.\nHence,\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s=0.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd9aad0a-f415-577c-9cb0-15044e4a43fa" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux in a Source-Free Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d564762-e4a4-5bb0-afe8-b8b8f6fac3d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516141} {:answer/id #uuid "b123ec58-423f-51d9-9afa-58d0e3e2907d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516480} {:answer/id #uuid "bb5bb2aa-9b98-5e14-a1e1-eb9f5bf08258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516506} {:answer/id #uuid "bc23addf-a23c-586d-8c9e-b7440595c852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516507} {:answer/id #uuid "be5c6d3f-c2f5-5f4b-a5b6-a35aad7bbaac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516513}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb5bb2aa-9b98-5e14-a1e1-eb9f5bf08258" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516506} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2781212-fedc-5b0a-8c49-342c3e418b01" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516735}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c428bbdb-3474-5c6d-a12b-bbe7007eab5d" :question/math-academy-id "q-162566" :question/problem "![](images/3f/3f84859100733c56d6381f6ae5c3f5fa55c693621190b3f921e3e1e8f850a4f9.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(1,1)$ and $B(-1,-1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=2x\\,i+(3-2y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(1-2t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(3-2y)=2-2=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/51/5186fb872f03e73124d04c2e9ff07e77fb336bef121484a9ff1b697ea0cf8bcd.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(1-2t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =2x=2(1-2t)=2-4t, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =3-2y=3-2(1-2t)=1+4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-4t)\\cdot (-2)-(1+4t)\\cdot (-2)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2-4t)-(1+4t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =-2{\\int }_{0}^{1}1-8t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot {[t-4{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (1-4) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=6.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516846 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198403 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd9aad0a-f415-577c-9cb0-15044e4a43fa" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Flux in a Source-Free Vector Field"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b595a3e-31c3-551c-aa15-2adbb1a368be" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516133} {:answer/id #uuid "41921c02-93c9-559a-a5a1-ba1cc7f58824" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516241} {:answer/id #uuid "88b0143e-7552-5d17-a22a-24748c27d899" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516398} {:answer/id #uuid "a6b23766-cc77-57ee-8748-4e55410f3e01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516459} {:answer/id #uuid "d99a11bb-26fc-599e-b35a-158a8e9b882d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516570}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d99a11bb-26fc-599e-b35a-158a8e9b882d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516570} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d787048-8743-53b0-8f95-62517b984fc0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516656}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bee8fa81-1bb7-5df3-bc77-fd80b7f5193e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162568" :question/problem "![](images/40/40e6a88e309f6ce5fece628a6d9bd66ffd00105edd3816fac04ae534ef7558b5.png)\n\nThe curve ${C}_{1}$ has endpoints $A(2,1)$ and $B(-1,-1).$ Calculate the flux of the vector field $F(x,y)=(4x+2y)\\,i-(8x+4y)\\,j$ through ${C}_{1}$ measured with respect to the unit normal vector ${n}_{1},$ as shown above.\n\n*Hint: You're given that $F$ is source-free, and the segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B$ can be parametrized as*\n\n$$\nr=(2-3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j$ is a vector field with simply connected domain $D⊆{R}^{2},$ then the following are equivalent:\n\n- $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\n- $∇\\cdot F=\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=0.$\n\n- The flux ${∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s$ along any closed curve $C\\in D$ is zero.\n\n- Flux is path-independent. That is, given any two curves ${C}_{1},{C}_{2}\\in D$ with the same start and endpoints, we have that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\n- There exists a stream function $ψ(x,y)$ for $F.$\n\nFirst, notice that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x+2y)+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-8x-4y))=4-4=0,\n$$\n\nwhich shows that $F$ is a source-free vector field.\n\nSince $F$ is source-free, we know the flux of $F$ is independent of the path. In particular, as we have that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{2}$ is the line segment $\\overset{―}{AB}$ traversed from $A$ to $B,$ and ${n}_{2}$ is the unit normal to ${C}_{2},$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/36/363ebd04d2629ed2b1b29ccab326e22737cf42e4c026d62910a50a1dd88e80f2.png)\n\nNotice that both ${n}_{1}$ and ${n}_{2}$ are ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotations of their respective unit tangent vectors.\n\nTo calculate the flux of $F$ across ${C}_{2},$ we will use the formula\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\,\\text{d}s={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTo apply the formula,\n\n- the curve should be parametrized as $r(t)$ for $t\\in [a,b],$ and\n\n- the unit normal vector ${n}_{2}$ *must* be a ${90}^{∘}$ clockwise rotation of the unit normal tangent vector $T(t).$\n\nWe have the parametrization\n\n$$\nr(t)=(2-3t)\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j,\\,t\\in [0,1].\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(x,y) & =4x+2y=4(2-3t)+2(1-2t)=-16t+10, \\\\ Q(x,y) & =-8x-4y=-8(2-3t)-4(1-2t)=32t-20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x(t)$ and $y(t)$ along the curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2-3t)=-3, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the flux integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot {n}_{2}\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{{C}_{2}}P\\,\\text{d}y-Q\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(P\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}-Q\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(-16t+10)\\cdot (-2)-(32t-20)\\cdot (-3)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(32t-20)+(96t-60)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}128t-80\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={[64{t}^{2}-80t]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =64-80 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot {n}_{1}\\,\\text{d}s=-16.\n$$"}]}
